WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.150
- [Narrator] At a hospital in
Modesto, California, the price

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.100
for a routine knee replacement surgery

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.100
might be about $16,000 or
$53,000 or even $81,000.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.040
How is a hospital able to charge

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.700
such an extreme range of
prices for the same surgery?

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.620
The vast price differences are driven

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.200
in part by the secret
rates hospitals negotiate

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.180
with health insurers, for everything

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.610
from an MRI to open-heart surgery.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:29.310
- It depends on the leverage
that the hospital has.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.920
It depends on the
leverage the insurer has.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.930
Depends on sort of who's
good at negotiating.

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:37.170
And until recently, those
rates tended to be secret.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.060
- [Narrator] But they're
not so secret anymore.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.490
Thanks to federal rules
that required hospitals

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:44.340
to make their prices public in 2021.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.860
Sutter Health is one of
hundreds of hospital systems

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.230
now revealing their negotiated rates.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:52.920
- People didn't know what
their competitors were paying

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.700
and that limited, the
negotiations in some ways.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.280
- [Narrator] So, how
exactly are these rates set?

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.950
Here's what goes into the
secret price negotiations

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.310
between hospitals and insurance companies.

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:08.550
To understand how U.S.
hospitals set their prices.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.830
Let's take a look at
publicly available data

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:13.170
from Sutter Health's
Memorial Medical Center.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:17.610
This chart shows the prices
for billing code DRG 470

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.490
which is a routine hip or
knee replacement surgery.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:22.920
The different price tags
depend on the insurance plan,

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:24.360
covering the procedure.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.000
If you look at the lowest
and most expensive rates,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.230
you can see there's a pricing
spread of more than $64,000.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:34.650
Still, the data doesn't represent
the full range of pricing.

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.930
The exact costs of certain insurance plans

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.210
are not available on
the hospital's website.

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.700
- One takeaway has definitely
been the broad range

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.190
of prices that are paid
for the same service

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:45.240
in the same hospital.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.400
You see this in Sutter's
data, but you see it

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.280
across the board in lots
of other hospitals' data.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.440
- [Narrator] So, how do
hospitals and insurance companies

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.150
come up with these numbers?

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.430
Hospitals typically have a sticker price,

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.560
often called the chargemaster rate.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.690
This can be a starting
point for negotiations

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.310
with insurance companies.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.800
At Sutter Health Memorial,
the chargemaster price

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.800
for a routine hip or knee
replacement is set at $92,262

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:11.910
but there isn't an exact formula

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.830
for how this cost is calculated.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:16.410
- A lot of factors influence
chargemaster prices.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.086
Hospitals typically will tweak them

00:02:18.086 --> 00:02:20.550
to meet their financial targets.

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:24.300
The chargemaster rates are
not typically really tied

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.190
to estimates of cost or value
for the service provided.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:32.400
Some hospitals are now trying
to drill down and say, "Okay,

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.720
how much does it really cost

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.640
to do this particular procedure?"

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.800
But that's not historically been the case.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.890
Insurers would rather have
rates that are not actually tied

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:43.650
to the chargemaster and
when they are negotiating

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:46.320
more successfully,
they're not paying a rate

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:47.910
that's a discount off the chargemaster,

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.370
they're paying a rate
set in some other way.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.860
- [Narrator] Hospitals will
often tweak chargemaster rates

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.900
based on their own financial targets.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.450
Then insurers will often
negotiate discounts

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:58.680
off these rates.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.350
- Insurance companies
tend to have more leverage

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:04.650
in the negotiations if they
can deliver a lot of patients.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.650
If they have a lot of
market share in that area.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:11.104
And also, if they're
willing to design a plan

00:03:11.104 --> 00:03:13.890
that has a limited number of hospitals

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:17.250
or a limited network so
that the hospital knows

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:19.020
that its competitors are not going to be

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:22.170
in that plan or not as
favored as they are.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:23.310
And in that case, a hospital

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.130
will tend to offer a better rate.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.500
- [Narrator] Insurers are
essentially buying in bulk

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.200
using their large membership
to get better deals.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.930
Meanwhile, prestigious
or large hospital systems

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:37.050
could have greater
leverage in negotiations.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.900
- What gives a hospital
leverage or negotiating cloud

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.840
is basically being a must have system,

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.060
meaning that the insurer really needs

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.970
to have that hospital
system to have a good plan

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:49.710
that will attract customers.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:53.130
In the markets where Sutter
Health has hospitals,

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.170
I think it would probably be difficult

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:58.500
for an insurer to create a strong network

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.080
that included no Sutter Health facilities.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.940
They have some important facilities

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:05.760
and they are a powerful
presence in their markets.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.350
- [Narrator] In a
statement, a spokesperson

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.990
from Sutter Health said, "It
is the insurance companies

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:12.180
not the health systems who hold leverage

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.500
in these negotiations.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.626
Sutter negotiates on
behalf of our patients

00:04:15.626 --> 00:04:18.780
to help ensure they can receive
access to the clinicians

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.480
and facilities who can meet
their healthcare needs".

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.970
Meanwhile, insurance
companies have generally said

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.040
their contract negotiations with hospitals

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:28.590
achieve large overall
savings for their members.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:31.470
And comparing individual
prices is not indicative

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:33.300
of cost-competitiveness.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.730
The full impact of the
contracts between hospitals

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.340
and insurers can be
difficult to see directly

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.880
because the details of the
negotiations often remain secret.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.360
For instance, some contracts
include restrictive provisions

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.030
sometimes called anti-steering clauses.

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:50.940
These clauses can prevent
insurers from directing patients

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:54.570
to less expensive or better
quality hospitals and doctors.

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:56.880
- Hospitals, when they
have a lot of leverage

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:58.200
in a very powerful in their market,

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:01.514
they can sometimes win
favorable contract terms

00:05:01.514 --> 00:05:05.040
so they could get provisions
in contracts that said

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.590
an insurer can't exclude their hospitals

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.870
from any of its plans,
that all of its plans

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:12.870
must include that hospital
systems, facilities.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:13.710
- [Narrator] Insurance plans

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.300
offered under government programs

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.867
like Medicare and Medicaid
tend to get the lowest rates

00:05:17.867 --> 00:05:20.490
because they're tied to prices mandated

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:22.620
by federal and state agencies.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:23.910
You can see this in the knee

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:26.670
and hip replacement surgery
prices we showed you earlier.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.980
- So, you'll see that
all of the Medicare plans

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:31.020
are clustered around
exactly that same number,

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:33.660
because it is related
to what Medicare itself

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:34.920
pays for that service.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.260
But then you'll see a big range of prices.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.600
You'll see some around $53,000.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:42.300
You see one as high as around $81,000.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.190
And those are the negotiated rates

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.364
that the different insurers are paying.

00:05:46.364 --> 00:05:48.810
- [Narrator] Because
Medicaid and Medicare plans

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:50.250
tend to get lower rates.

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:52.140
Some hospitals say they need to rely

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:54.690
on the higher paying
privately insured patients

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:55.830
for their revenue.

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:59.070
- There was a study that looked
at more than 2,800 hospitals

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:00.780
over 10 years.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:04.350
And they found that hospitals
that boosted their margins

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:06.090
didn't cut their costs.

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:09.000
They actually improved their
revenue by raising the rates

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:11.430
they charged to private insurers.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:13.177
- [Narrator] A spokesperson
from Sutter Health said,

00:06:13.177 --> 00:06:15.630
"The rates paid by
Medicaid and Medicare plans

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:18.330
generally don't cover the costs of care".

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:20.640
The prices, health insurance
companies negotiate

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.230
affect consumers directly.

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:24.720
Throughout of pocket
charges like deductibles

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.260
and indirectly by pushing up
premiums for health coverage.

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:30.270
For instance, high deductible plans

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:31.740
can leave patients responsible

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:33.570
for thousands of dollars in costs

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:35.340
before their coverage kicks in.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:37.260
Total spending on private health insurance

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:40.830
in the U.S. has increased by
about 40% over the past decade,

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:42.660
according to federal figures.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.300
- Patients who don't
have any insurance at all

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.580
can sometimes be asked to pay a cash price

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:51.330
that is higher than what
insurers pay it varies.

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:53.310
But some of these patients may also,

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.470
if they're low income,
get financial assistance

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:57.990
in which case they get,
discounts based on that,

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:00.330
or may have the whole bill forgiven.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:02.700
- [Narrator] Despite the federal
rules requiring hospitals

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:04.200
to make their prices public,

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:07.530
some hospitals still haven't
fully revealed their rates.

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:09.330
On July 1st, the second phase

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:12.300
of the federal pricing
transparency effort took effect.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.700
Health insurers now have to
reveal the prices they pay

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:17.700
to all healthcare providers,
not just hospitals

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:21.030
but also doctor groups,
surgery centers, and labs.

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:23.100
- When the federal government
created these rules,

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:27.480
one of the ideas was to help
people shop for services.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.060
That's one question, will
people look at these prices

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:32.400
and try to shop for care based on them?

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:33.870
The belief is that now

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:36.540
that those prices are
becoming more transparent,

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:39.180
we'll see a narrower range of prices

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:42.544
because insurers and
hospitals both will say, "Hey,

00:07:42.544 --> 00:07:44.850
I see that other person's
getting a better rate.

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:45.810
I want that rate".

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:48.552
And that will tend to
narrow the big variation.

00:07:48.552 --> 00:07:51.635
(light gentle music)

